        });
        System.exit(report());
    }
    // ─── end tests ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
}

fund-java/20-jpa-transactions-lazy-loadi.explanation:
Deduplicate with a `LinkedHashSet`, slice it into batches, merge every batch's result into one map, then build the rows:

```java
var ids = new ArrayList<>(new LinkedHashSet<>(orders.stream().map(Order::customerId).toList()));
var customers = new HashMap<Long, Customer>();
for (int i = 0; i < ids.size(); i += batchSize) {
    customers.putAll(loadCustomers.apply(new LinkedHashSet<>(ids.subList(i, Math.min(i + batchSize, ids.size())))));
}
return orders.stream()
    .map(o -> new OrderRow(o.id(), Optional.ofNullable(customers.get(o.customerId())).map(Customer::name).orElse("(unknown)")))
    .toList();
```

This is exactly what Hibernate does for a lazy `@ManyToOne` with a batch size: one query per batch instead of one per row.

Skipping the dedupe, rather than going through a `LinkedHashSet`, sends customer 1 twice: three calls, and "distinct ids are loaded in batches" fails. A `HashSet` instead of the `LinkedHashSet` loses the first-seen order, which the same test checks with customers 30, 10 and 20. A loop that stops at full batches (`i + batchSize <= ids.size()`) drops the remainder, so "the number of calls rounds up" and "rows keep the order sequence" fail. Reading `customers.get(id).name()` without a fallback throws a NullPointerException, and "a missing customer gets a placeholder" fails.

