fund-java/20-jpa-transactions-lazy-loadi.explanation:
An `InvocationHandler` that owns the transaction only when it started it:

```java
InvocationHandler handler = (proxy, method, args) -> {
    boolean owner = method.isAnnotationPresent(Transactional.class) && !tx.active;
    if (owner) tx.begin();
    Object result;
    try {
        result = method.invoke(target, args);
    } catch (InvocationTargetException e) {
        Throwable cause = e.getCause();
        if (owner) { if (cause instanceof RuntimeException || cause instanceof Error) tx.rollback(); else tx.commit(); }
        throw cause;
    }
    if (owner) tx.commit();
    return result;
};
return iface.cast(Proxy.newProxyInstance(iface.getClassLoader(), new Class<?>[] { iface }, handler));
```

The tests catch three plausible wrong versions. One that rolls back on every exception, rather than only on unchecked ones, fails "a checked exception COMMITS by default" (a commit is Spring's default there). One that rethrows the `InvocationTargetException` instead of its cause fails "a runtime exception rolls back and reaches the caller unwrapped", because the caller of `placeOrder` gets an `UndeclaredThrowableException`; it fails "a checked exception COMMITS by default" too, since `refund` declares `throws Exception` and so passes the wrapper on, without the bank's message. One that begins a new transaction for every annotated call fails "a nested @Transactional call joins the active transaction (REQUIRED)" with two begins. The self-invocation test passes with any correct proxy: it documents that no proxy can see a call the target makes on `this`.

